--- DESCRIPTION ---
A guide to the structure of the skeletal, muscular, cardiovascular and respiratory systems for sport and exercise.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Body Systems Matter in Sport</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every movement you make in sport — a sprint finish, a tackle, a shot at goal — depends on at least four body systems working in harmony. Sport scientists, physiotherapists and coaches all need a solid understanding of how these systems are structured before they can make sense of how the body responds to exercise. This lesson focuses on the <dfn class="term" data-def="A group of organs and tissues that work together to carry out a specific function in the body.">body system</dfn> structures you are required to know: the skeletal system, the muscular system, the cardiovascular system and the respiratory system.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The Skeletal System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The skeleton is the body's rigid framework. It is made up of 206 bones in an adult and is divided into two main parts: the axial skeleton (skull, vertebral column, ribcage) and the appendicular skeleton (limbs, shoulder girdle, pelvis). Bones are classified by their shape. <strong>Long bones</strong> — such as the femur, humerus, tibia and fibula — act as levers during movement. <strong>Short bones</strong> (wrist and ankle bones) provide stability and support. <strong>Flat bones</strong> — the cranium, scapula, ribs and pelvis — protect vital organs. <strong>Irregular bones</strong> include the vertebrae of the vertebral column. The main bones you must know are: the cranium (skull), clavicle, scapula, humerus, radius, ulna, ribs, vertebral column, pelvis, femur, patella (kneecap), tibia and fibula.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and try to recall the full list of main bones from the spec. Work from head to foot: cranium, scapula, humerus, radius, ulna, ribs, vertebral column, pelvis, femur, patella, tibia, fibula.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The main bones you must know are: cranium, scapula, humerus, radius, ulna, ribs, vertebral column, pelvis, femur, patella, tibia and fibula. Work from head to toe and you will not miss any.</p>
</div>

<h3 data-narration-id="n6">Types of Joint and Synovial Joint Structure</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Where two or more bones meet, a <dfn class="term" data-def="The point at which two or more bones meet; in synovial joints, the joint is freely moveable and lubricated by synovial fluid.">synovial joint</dfn> allows free movement. All synovial joints share the same basic structure: a <strong>joint capsule</strong> encloses the joint, lined by the <strong>synovial membrane</strong> which secretes <strong>synovial fluid</strong> to lubricate and nourish the articular cartilage. <strong>Articular (hyaline) cartilage</strong> covers the ends of bones, reducing friction. <strong>Ligaments</strong> connect bone to bone, stabilising the joint. The three synovial joint types you need to know are: <strong>ball and socket</strong> joints (shoulder and hip — allow movement in all planes), <strong>hinge</strong> joints (knee and elbow — allow flexion and extension only) and <strong>pivot</strong> joints (top of the vertebral column/atlas and axis — allow rotation).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What are the key structures inside a synovial joint?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">A synovial joint contains six key structures that work together to allow smooth, pain-free movement. The joint capsule is a tough fibrous sleeve enclosing the whole joint. Inside it, the synovial membrane produces synovial fluid — a viscous liquid that lubricates and delivers nutrients to the articular cartilage. Articular cartilage (hyaline cartilage) coats the bone surfaces, reducing friction and absorbing compressive forces during impact. Ligaments are strong bands of connective tissue that hold bone to bone, preventing dislocation. Some joints also contain bursae (fluid-filled sacs) to cushion areas of friction, and in the knee, fibrocartilage menisci deepen the joint surface and distribute load.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">The Muscular System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">There are three types of muscle tissue in the body. <strong>Cardiac muscle</strong> is found only in the heart and contracts involuntarily and continuously throughout life. <strong>Smooth muscle</strong> lines internal organs such as blood vessels and the digestive tract and also works involuntarily. <strong>Skeletal (voluntary) muscle</strong> is attached to bones via tendons and is under conscious control — it is the type of muscle used in all voluntary sporting movements.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n11">The major voluntary muscles you must locate are: <strong>biceps</strong> (front of upper arm — flexes the elbow), <strong>triceps</strong> (rear of upper arm — extends the elbow), <strong>deltoids</strong> (top of the shoulder — abducts the arm), <strong>pectorals/pectoralis major</strong> (chest — adducts and rotates the arm), <strong>abdominals</strong> (abdomen — flex the trunk and stabilise the core), <strong>quadriceps</strong> (front of the thigh — extends the knee), <strong>hamstrings</strong> (rear of the thigh — flexes the knee), <strong>gastrocnemius</strong> (calf — plantarflexes the ankle), <strong>gluteals/gluteus maximus</strong> (buttocks — extends and externally rotates the hip), and <strong>latissimus dorsi</strong> (broad muscle of the back — pulls the arm downward and backward). The spec also includes <strong>trapezius</strong> (upper back and neck — elevates and retracts the scapula).</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Cover the list and recall each muscle's location and the joint it acts on. Examiners often give a sporting action and ask you to name the prime mover.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The major muscle groups are: biceps, triceps, deltoids, pectorals, abdominals, quadriceps, hamstrings, gastrocnemius, gluteus maximus, latissimus dorsi and trapezius. For each one, know its location on the body.</p>
</div>

<h3 data-narration-id="n13">Muscle Fibre Types</h3>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Skeletal muscle fibres are not all identical. The specification distinguishes two main types. <dfn class="term" data-def="Slow-twitch muscle fibres that are highly resistant to fatigue, rely on aerobic respiration and are recruited for low-intensity, long-duration activities such as distance running.">Type I (slow-twitch) fibres</dfn> contract slowly, generate less force but are very resistant to fatigue. They are rich in mitochondria and myoglobin, giving them a red appearance, and they rely predominantly on aerobic respiration. They are well suited to endurance activities such as marathon running or cycling. <strong>Type II (fast-twitch) fibres</strong> contract rapidly and generate greater force but fatigue quickly. They rely more on anaerobic respiration and are recruited during high-intensity, short-duration activities such as sprinting or weightlifting.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">The Cardiovascular System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The cardiovascular system consists of the heart, blood vessels and blood. The <strong>heart</strong> is a four-chambered pump located in the chest cavity, slightly left of centre. The <strong>right atrium</strong> receives deoxygenated blood from the body via the <strong>vena cava</strong>. From there it passes through the tricuspid valve into the <strong>right ventricle</strong>, which pumps it to the lungs via the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>. Oxygenated blood returns from the lungs through the <strong>pulmonary vein</strong> into the <strong>left atrium</strong>, then passes through the mitral (bicuspid) valve into the <strong>left ventricle</strong> — the most powerful chamber — which pumps blood around the entire body through the <strong>aorta</strong>. The left ventricle has much thicker walls than the right because it must generate higher pressure for systemic circulation.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n17">Blood travels through three types of vessel. <dfn class="term" data-def="Blood vessels that carry oxygenated blood away from the heart to the body's tissues; they have thick, elastic, muscular walls to withstand high pressure.">Arteries</dfn> carry blood away from the heart at high pressure; their thick, elastic walls absorb the surge of pressure from each heartbeat. <strong>Veins</strong> carry blood back towards the heart at low pressure; they have thinner walls and contain valves to prevent backflow. <strong>Capillaries</strong> are microscopic, single-cell-thick vessels that form networks within tissues, allowing the exchange of oxygen, carbon dioxide and nutrients between the blood and cells. Blood itself is composed of red blood cells (carry oxygen via haemoglobin), white blood cells (immune defence), platelets (clotting) and plasma (the liquid carrying nutrients and waste).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How do arteries, veins and capillaries differ in structure?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Arteries have three distinct wall layers: a tough outer adventitia, a thick middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic tissue (tunica media), and a smooth inner endothelium. This construction allows them to expand and recoil with each heartbeat (the pulse you feel at your wrist is an artery). Veins share the same three layers but their tunica media is much thinner — they operate at low pressure and rely on skeletal muscle contractions and one-way valves to return blood to the heart. Capillaries are just one cell thick (endothelium only), with no muscle or connective tissue layers. Their tiny diameter (7–10 micrometres) means red blood cells pass through in single file, maximising contact time with surrounding cells for efficient gas and nutrient exchange.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">The Respiratory System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">The respiratory system draws oxygen into the body and expels carbon dioxide. Air enters through the nose and mouth and passes down the <strong>larynx</strong> (voice box) into the <dfn class="term" data-def="The airway running from the larynx to the bronchi; a rigid tube held open by C-shaped rings of cartilage.">trachea</dfn> (windpipe), a rigid tube kept open by C-shaped rings of cartilage. At the base of the trachea the airway divides into the left and right <strong>bronchi</strong> (singular: bronchus), each leading into its respective lung. Inside each lung the bronchi branch repeatedly into progressively narrower <strong>bronchioles</strong>, ending in tiny air sacs called <strong>alveoli</strong>.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n21">The <strong>alveoli</strong> are the functional units of gas exchange: their enormous combined surface area (approximately 70 m² in an adult), moist thin walls and rich capillary network allow oxygen to diffuse from air into blood and carbon dioxide to diffuse from blood into air. The two <strong>lungs</strong> sit within the ribcage; the right lung has three lobes and the left lung has two (to accommodate the heart). Breathing itself is driven by the <strong>diaphragm</strong> — a dome-shaped sheet of skeletal muscle beneath the lungs. When it contracts and flattens, it increases thoracic volume, drawing air in (inspiration). When it relaxes and rises back, thoracic volume decreases, forcing air out (expiration). The <strong>intercostal muscles</strong> between the ribs assist this process by lifting and lowering the ribcage.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The skeletal system consists of 206 bones, classified as long, short, flat or irregular; the main bones run from cranium to fibula. Synovial joints (ball and socket, hinge, pivot) allow free movement and share a common structure of capsule, synovial membrane, articular cartilage and ligaments.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">The major voluntary muscles include the biceps, triceps, deltoids, pectorals, abdominals, quadriceps, hamstrings, gastrocnemius, gluteus maximus, latissimus dorsi and trapezius. Muscle fibres are either Type I (slow-twitch, aerobic, fatigue-resistant) or Type II (fast-twitch, anaerobic, powerful but fatigable).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">The heart has four chambers (right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle); blood flows through arteries, capillaries and veins, and blood is composed of red cells, white cells, platelets and plasma.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">The respiratory system moves air through the trachea, bronchi, bronchioles and into the alveoli where gas exchange takes place; breathing is controlled by the diaphragm and intercostal muscles.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">Structural questions often ask you to name a specific part of a body system or to label a diagram. Learn the correct anatomical terms from the required list — for example, write "vena cava" not "large vein into the heart", and "gastrocnemius" not "calf muscle". For joint questions, always state the joint type and give an example location. For questions about the heart, remember the direction of blood flow: body → right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → body. Drawing and labelling this pathway from memory is an excellent revision strategy.</p>

