  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): Detailed explanation of both fibre types with structural reasons linked clearly to sporting demands. For example: Type I (slow-twitch) fibres are rich in mitochondria and myoglobin, enabling sustained aerobic energy production — well suited to endurance activities such as a goalkeeper's repeated repositioning or a midfielder's 90-minute work rate. Type II (fast-twitch) fibres lack high mitochondrial density but have rapid cross-bridge cycling and access to large anaerobic energy stores, enabling explosive short bursts such as a sprint to the ball. The answer links structural features (mitochondria, contraction speed) to function and gives sporting examples.\nSecure (3-4 marks): Both types identified and their general characteristics described, with some link to sport.\nDeveloping (2 marks): One type described with limited sporting link.\nEmerging (1 mark): A basic statement about fast and slow fibres with no structural detail. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A sport scientist is assessing two athletes: a long-distance runner and a javelin thrower. Using your knowledge of the structure of the skeletal, muscular and cardiovascular systems, evaluate how the structural features of these systems combine to support performance in each athlete's chosen sport.",
