=== L3 Short-Term Effects of Exercise ===
id: 7d6d54c2-8d0c-433d-8a06-656ed00f18d2
slug: lesson-03
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:38:48.027841+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 20

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Immediate changes to your cardiovascular, respiratory and muscular systems during a single exercise session.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Does Your Body Change During Exercise?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The moment you begin exercising, your body's systems react almost instantly to match the increased demand for energy and oxygen. These immediate responses — which reverse quickly once you stop — are called the <dfn class="term" data-def="The immediate, temporary changes that happen to the body's systems during a single exercise session, which return to resting levels shortly after exercise stops.">short-term effects of exercise</dfn>. Understanding them is essential for any coach or athlete who wants to explain why warm-ups, pacing and hydration matter so much in practice. The key systems affected are the cardiovascular system, the cardio-respiratory system, and the muscular-skeletal system.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Cardiovascular Changes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Your heart is a pump, and during exercise it needs to deliver far more oxygen-rich blood to your working muscles. It achieves this in three linked ways. First, your <dfn class="term" data-def="The number of times the heart beats per minute; it rises during exercise to pump more blood to the working muscles.">heart rate</dfn> increases — the number of beats per minute rises from a typical resting rate of around 60-80 bpm to well over 150 bpm during intense activity. Second, <dfn class="term" data-def="The volume of blood pumped out of the heart with each beat; it increases during exercise as the heart muscle contracts more forcefully.">stroke volume</dfn> — the amount of blood pushed out per beat — also increases because adrenaline and returning venous blood cause the heart to contract more forcefully. Third, these two changes multiply to produce a much higher <dfn class="term" data-def="The total volume of blood the heart pumps per minute, calculated by multiplying heart rate by stroke volume (HR × SV).">cardiac output</dfn>, calculated as heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. A trained athlete can increase cardiac output from roughly 5 litres per minute at rest to over 20 litres per minute during maximal exercise.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5">Another important cardiovascular short-term effect is the redistribution of blood flow. At rest, blood is shared fairly evenly across all the organs. During exercise, blood vessels supplying the working muscles <strong>vasodilate</strong> (widen), while vessels to less active organs such as the digestive system <strong>vasoconstrict</strong> (narrow). This redirection ensures that the muscles receiving the hardest workload get the greatest share of oxygen and nutrients at the time they are needed most.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Remember the cardiac output formula: HR x SV = CO. If the exam gives you heart rate and stroke volume figures, multiply them together. Units are beats per minute x litres per beat = litres per minute.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Cardiac output = Heart rate × Stroke volume. During intense exercise, both heart rate and stroke volume increase, so cardiac output rises dramatically. Blood is simultaneously redirected (vasodilation) towards working muscles and away from non-essential organs (vasoconstriction).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Cardio-Respiratory Changes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Your muscles need more oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide during exercise. The cardio-respiratory system responds by increasing both the rate and depth of breathing. <strong>Breathing frequency</strong> (breaths per minute) rises sharply — from around 12-20 breaths per minute at rest to 40-60 during hard exercise. At the same time, <dfn class="term" data-def="The volume of air inhaled or exhaled in a single breath; it increases during exercise as the respiratory muscles work harder to draw in more air.">tidal volume</dfn> — the amount of air moved in a single breath — increases because the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract more powerfully. Together, a higher breathing rate and a greater tidal volume produce a large increase in <strong>minute ventilation</strong> (the total volume of air breathed per minute), ensuring that oxygen supply and carbon dioxide removal keep pace with the energy demands of the working muscles.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n9">Increased body temperature is an inevitable by-product of the chemical reactions that release energy during exercise. The cardiovascular system helps manage this through vasodilation of blood vessels near the skin, allowing more heat to radiate away. <strong>Sweating</strong> is the other key cooling mechanism: sweat glands release water onto the skin surface, which evaporates and carries heat away from the body. This is why staying hydrated during exercise is so important — losing too much fluid through sweat reduces blood volume, which in turn makes it harder for the heart to maintain cardiac output.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="The two changes to breathing are rate (breaths per minute goes up) and depth (tidal volume goes up). Both must happen together to boost minute ventilation enough to meet the oxygen demand of exercise. A common exam mistake is to only mention one.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Minute ventilation increases during exercise because breathing rate AND tidal volume both rise. Body temperature also increases, triggering vasodilation near the skin and sweating — the body's main mechanisms for temperature regulation during exercise.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Muscular-Skeletal Changes and Muscle Fatigue</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">As exercise intensity rises, the working muscles receive an increased supply of oxygen and nutrients thanks to vasodilation and the higher cardiac output described above. The muscles also become warmer, which temporarily increases their elasticity — they can stretch and contract more efficiently, reducing injury risk. However, if exercise is intense or prolonged, the muscles eventually begin to fatigue. <strong>Muscle fatigue</strong> is the inability to maintain the required force output, and athletes experience it as a burning sensation, a feeling of heaviness, and a progressive loss of power and coordination.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n13">At high exercise intensities, when the cardio-respiratory system cannot deliver oxygen quickly enough to match demand, the muscles switch to <strong>anaerobic respiration</strong>. One waste product of this process is <strong>lactic acid</strong>. As lactic acid accumulates in the muscle tissue and bloodstream, it reduces the ability of the muscle fibres to contract effectively, contributing to the burning sensation and fatigue. The point at which lactic acid accumulates faster than the body can remove it is sometimes called the lactate threshold — going beyond this threshold rapidly accelerates fatigue. Lactic acid is eventually cleared during recovery through increased breathing and blood flow, which is one reason a gentle cool-down is important after intense exercise.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does the body clear lactic acid after exercise?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Once exercise intensity drops, the body enters a recovery phase in which oxygen consumption remains elevated — known informally as excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC). During this period, increased breathing rate and blood flow deliver extra oxygen to the muscles, which is used to convert the accumulated lactic acid back into glucose in the liver (the Cori cycle). This process is why heart rate and breathing rate stay elevated for several minutes after exercise has stopped, and why a light cool-down jog is more effective than stopping abruptly — it keeps blood flowing and speeds lactic acid removal.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How do the short-term effects vary with exercise intensity?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">At low intensities (e.g. a brisk walk), heart rate and breathing rate rise modestly, energy is supplied almost entirely aerobically, and lactic acid stays below the lactate threshold. At moderate intensities (e.g. a steady run), heart rate and tidal volume are noticeably elevated, sweating begins, and muscles start to warm up significantly. At high intensities (e.g. a 400m sprint), cardiac output is near its maximum, breathing rate and tidal volume are at peak, lactic acid accumulates rapidly, and muscle fatigue sets in quickly. The exam may ask you to compare the responses at different intensities — always link the cardiovascular, respiratory and muscular systems together in your answer for higher marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">The cardiovascular system responds immediately to exercise by increasing heart rate, stroke volume and cardiac output, and by redirecting blood flow to working muscles via vasodilation.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">The cardio-respiratory system increases both breathing rate (frequency) and tidal volume (depth) to raise minute ventilation and meet the body's greater oxygen demand.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Body temperature rises during exercise, triggering sweating and vasodilation near the skin to regulate heat; muscles become more elastic but fatigue sets in as lactic acid accumulates at higher intensities.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">A common exam question asks you to describe or explain the short-term effects of exercise on a named body system. Always be specific: if the question is about the cardiovascular system, you should mention heart rate, stroke volume and cardiac output (and state that CO = HR × SV). If it is about the cardio-respiratory system, mention both breathing rate and tidal volume — not just one. For higher-mark questions, link systems together: increased cardiac output helps supply the muscles, which in turn allows continued aerobic respiration and delays lactic acid build-up. Applied questions will give you a scenario (e.g. a footballer in the 80th minute of a match) — always refer back to that scenario when you explain fatigue or sweating.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
